1. DevOps Tools & Concepts 
In the file explorer image, several core DevOps tools are listed. Here is what they are used for:

Ansible: Configuration management. It automates the setup of software on many servers at once.

Terraform: Infrastructure as Code (IaC). Used to "spin up" resources like VPCs and EC2 instances on AWS.

Docker: Containerization. It packages an application with all its dependencies so it runs the same everywhere.

Kubernetes (K8s): Container orchestration. It manages clusters of Docker containers, handling scaling and self-healing.

Jenkins: Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD). It automates the building, testing, and deployment of code.

ECR (Elastic Container Registry): A private place on AWS to store and manage your Docker images.

2. Understanding chmod 722
Linux permissions are divided into three sections (User, Group, Others) and three types (Read=4, Write=2, Execute=1).

The Breakdown of 722:
Section	Digit	Binary Calculation	Permissions	Meaning
Owner (User)	7	4 (R) + 2 (W) + 1 (X)	rwx	Can do everything.
Group	2	0 (R) + 2 (W) + 0 (X)	-w-	Can only write (no read/execute).
Others	2	0 (R) + 2 (W) + 0 (X)	-w-	Can only write (no read/execute).
Warning: Using 722 is very rare and usually insecure, as others can modify your file but cannot even see what is in it.

3. Vulnerability Scanning & Self-Destruction
How to know if a Docker image is vulnerable:
You use Scanners that compare your image layers against a database of known vulnerabilities (CVEs).

Trivy: The industry favorite. Run trivy image <image_name>.

Snyk: Great for developer-friendly reports. Run snyk container test <image_name>.

Docker Scout: Built directly into Docker Desktop and CLI.

Creating a Self-Destroying EC2:
In your interview step 15, you need to ensure no resources remain. You can automate this using:

TTL (Time to Live) Script: Use a cron job or a at command in the User Data script that runs sudo shutdown -h +60 (shuts down in 60 mins).

Terraform: Use terraform destroy -auto-approve at the end of your pipeline.

4. Docker & Jenkins Pipeline
Here is a Dockerfile and a Jenkinsfile snippet to handle the workflow you described (Build → Scan → Push → Deploy).

Dockerfile (Ubuntu + Python)
Dockerfile
FROM ubuntu:22.04
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y python3
WORKDIR /app
COPY hello.py .
# Run the script when the container starts
CMD ["python3", "hello.py"]
Jenkins Pipeline (CI/CD with Security Gate)
This pipeline logic includes the "Critical vs Low" vulnerability check you requested:

Groovy
pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Build & Scan') {
            steps {
                sh 'docker build -t my-app:latest .'
                // Run Trivy scan and save result. 
                // --exit-code 1 makes it fail the build if CRITICAL is found.
                sh 'trivy image --severity CRITICAL --exit-code 1 my-app:latest'
                // If the above passes, it means no CRITICALS were found.
            }
        }
        stage('Push to ECR') {
            steps {
                sh 'aws ecr get-login-password --region mumbai | docker login ...'
                sh 'docker push <aws_account_id>.dkr.ecr.mumbai.amazonaws.com/my-app:latest'
            }
        }
        stage('Deploy to K8s') {
            steps {
                sh 'kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml'
            }
        }
    }
}
